Difference in means is consistent for a marginalised estimand under unknown interference \citep{savje2021average}; misspecified exposure mappings separate the estimand from the assumption \citep{leung2022causal,savje2023causal}; \citet{yu2022estimating} estimate the total effect without the network. Analyses of interference within groups \citep{liu2014large}, with local dependence \citep{leung2020treatment} and under random-graph models \citep{sussman2017elements,li2022random,hu2022average} are related background; we do not compare with them. Our setting fixes the estimand to the global effect and lets the response model be wrong.

\paragraph{Designs.}
Cluster-based, correlated and staggered-rollout designs \citep{ugander2013graph,ugander2023randomized,candogan2023correlated,cortez2022staggered,chen2023optimized} change the distribution of exposures the regression sees (see also \citealp{gui2015network,saveski2017detecting,basse2018designs,leung2022rate}); we cross the estimator with Bernoulli and graph-cluster assignment. The analogy to limited overlap \citep{crump2009dealing,damour2021overlap} is the source of our extrapolation framing.
