\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the results say.}
Regression beats DM when the spillover share is large enough that DM's bias dominates (78\% of the grid at $\sigma=0.5$) and the response is smooth in exposure. Its variance is predictable from the leverage of the corner contrast; its bias is set by the response between the observed exposure range and the corners, which the data inside that range do not identify. Two tools we hoped to offer did not work: no data-computable score we tried predicts the bias better than a constant, and the nominal 95\% interval covers in 74\% of Bernoulli cells, mostly because it ignores the bias.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Simulated outcomes.} All outcomes come from six mechanisms of our choosing, four smooth in exposure, with Gaussian noise; real graphs supply only structure. The shares of cells in which regression wins are shares of this grid, and choice rules are evaluated on the cells they were examined on. \emph{Graph range.} The $d$ exponent rests on three degrees at $n=3000$; thinning real graphs changes clustering and the degree tail with $d$. \emph{Estimators.} Causal message passing, graph neural networks and cluster Horvitz--Thompson were not run; the adaptive H\'ajek estimator is simplified; the flexible learners use 200 draws per graph and fixed hyperparameters. \emph{Design.} Mechanism (e) at $s=0.9$ is truncated at $K=30$ steps; $\sigma=2,4$ were added after the freeze. In mechanism (e) the exact bias prediction misses the Monte Carlo interval in 42\% of cells, for a reason we did not diagnose.

\paragraph{Implications.}
Within this scope: report the leverage $L$ and the estimated spillover share with the estimate, as they bear on the variance and on whether DM is better; do not read agreement with richer specifications as evidence of small bias, or rely on the default interval when the response may be curved or thresholded. Whether a bias bound from data in the observed exposure range can exist is open.
